{"id": "38935", "revid": "2278750", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=38935", "title": "Western Illinois", "text": "Western Illinois is a region of Illinois generally bounded by the Mississippi River to the west, I-80 to the north and I-39/I-55 to the east. It has two major anchors: Peoria and the Illinois side of the Quad Cities. Being culturally similar to Central Illinois, they are sometimes presented together as the same region.\nUnderstand.\nWestern Illinois is generally defined as the area west of I-39, south of I-80 and north of Metro-East. Much of the land in the region is dedicated to agriculture but there are several small towns that dot the region.\nCulturally, the area is similar to neighboring Central Illinois and Southeast Iowa. The people are friendly but do not particularly care for Chicago.\nGet around.\nThe best way to travel throughout the region is by car. I-74 is the primary thoroughfare and connects anchor cities Peoria and Rock Island. It also provides service to the Illinois Twin Cities (Bloomington and Normal) and the Urbana and Champaign area. US 24 serves as a secondary thoroughfare and connects Peoria to Quincy another anchor city located on the Mississippi. Interstate 72 also provides some service to the southern portions of the region."}
{"id": "38937", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=38937", "title": "Western India", "text": " \"Not to be confused with the West Indies, also known as the Caribbean.\"\nWestern India comprises three large states, one small state and one small union territory. It is bounded by Pakistan and the Arabian Sea to its west and the Gangetic Plains to its east. This is the most heterogeneous of India's regions, with the states differing dramatically from one another in language, culture and levels of economic development. Maharashtra and Gujarat are among the most industrialised states of India, while Rajasthan and Goa are magnets for tourists, though for different reasons.\nRegions.\nWestern India consists of four states and one union territory:\nKarnataka is culturally close to Maharashtra and Goa, but it has been included in Southern India due to its more difficult language barrier compared to Maharashtra or Goa.\nCities.\nHere are nine of the most notable cities.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nWestern India has one of the richest and most varied historical landscapes in the subcontinent, shaped by desert empires, maritime trade networks, warrior confederacies, and centuries of cultural exchange. Its story begins with the Indus Valley Civilization, which flourished in parts of present\u2011day Gujarat. Sites like Lothal reveal sophisticated urban planning, bead\u2011making industries, and one of the world\u2019s earliest known dockyards, underscoring the region\u2019s ancient maritime orientation. As Vedic culture spread across the subcontinent, the western plains and coasts became home to early kingdoms, pastoral communities, and trade guilds that linked India with Persia, Arabia, and the Mediterranean.\nFrom the early medieval period onward, Rajasthan emerged as the land of the Rajputs, whose clans established powerful kingdoms such as Mewar, Marwar, Amber, and Bikaner. These states built formidable hill forts, intricate palaces, and walled cities that still dominate the landscape. Rajput rulers cultivated a strong martial ethos, celebrated in epic poetry and folklore, and often resisted invasions from the Delhi Sultanate and the Mughals. Yet they also forged alliances, intermarried with imperial families, and became patrons of art, architecture, and literature. Their legacy remains central to the identity of Rajasthan.\nGujarat followed a different trajectory, shaped by its long coastline and thriving ports. Ancient maritime trade connected Gujarat to the Roman world, East Africa, and Southeast Asia. Over time, the region saw the rise of Hindu dynasties, Jain merchant guilds, and later Islamic sultanates centred in cities like Ahmedabad and Surat. Arab traders settled along the coast from the 7th century onward, contributing to the region\u2019s cosmopolitan character. Gujarat became a hub of textile production, shipbuilding, and commerce, attracting traders from across the Indian Ocean. It also played a major role in India\u2019s intellectual and political history as the birthplace of Mahatma Gandhi and a centre of the independence movement.\nMaharashtra\u2019s history is defined by the rise of the Maratha Empire, which reshaped the political map of India in the 17th and 18th centuries. Under the leadership of Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj, the Marathas developed innovative military strategies, built an extensive network of hill forts, and challenged Mughal dominance. After Shivaji, the Maratha Confederacy expanded across much of the subcontinent, becoming one of the most powerful forces of the era. The Deccan region also nurtured rich cultural traditions, including the Bhakti movement, classical music, and Marathi literature.\nThe western coastline became a battleground for European powers from the 16th century onward. The Portuguese established control over Goa, Daman, Diu, and parts of the Konkan coast, leaving a lasting Indo\u2011Portuguese cultural imprint. Their rule introduced new architectural styles, Christianity, and global trade connections. Meanwhile, the British East India Company consolidated power in Bombay (now Mumbai), transforming it into a major port and commercial hub. The city grew rapidly through trade, textile mills, and migration, eventually becoming the financial capital of India.\nAfter independence, Western India underwent significant political and administrative changes. Bombay State was reorganized along linguistic lines, leading to the creation of Maharashtra and Gujarat in 1960. Goa, Daman and Diu, and Dadra and Nagar Haveli were integrated into India after the end of Portuguese rule. Today, Western India reflects a blend of ancient civilizations, royal traditions, colonial legacies, and modern economic dynamism, making it one of the most historically layered regions in the country.\nGeography.\nWestern India spans an extraordinary range of landscapes\u2014from the golden dunes of the Thar Desert to the lush forests of the Western Ghats and the palm\u2011fringed beaches of Goa. \nRajasthan dominates the northwest with its arid plains, salt lakes, and rugged Aravalli hills, one of the world\u2019s oldest mountain ranges. The Thar Desert stretches across the state, dotted with oasis towns, camel routes, and dramatic sand dunes.\nGujarat\u2019s geography is shaped by its long coastline along the Arabian Sea, the marshy expanses of the Rann of Kutch, and fertile plains fed by rivers like the Narmada and Tapi. The Rann, with its seasonal salt flats, is one of India\u2019s most unique landscapes, home to migratory birds, wild asses, and striking white deserts that glow under the moonlight.\nMaharashtra is geographically diverse, with the Western Ghats (Sahyadri) forming a dramatic escarpment that separates the coastal Konkan region from the Deccan Plateau. The Ghats are a biodiversity hotspot, filled with waterfalls, forests, and hill stations like Mahabaleshwar and Matheran. The interior plateau is home to major cities such as Pune, Nagpur and Aurangabad, as well as ancient cave complexes like Ajanta and Ellora a UNESCO World Heritage Site. \nGoa, and the union territory of Dadra and Nagar Haveli and Daman and Diu add tropical coastlines, river estuaries, and forested hills to the region\u2019s varied terrain.\nClimate.\nWestern India\u2019s climate varies widely due to its diverse geography. Rajasthan experiences an extreme desert climate, with scorching summers, cool winters, and minimal rainfall. The monsoon brings some relief, but large parts of the state remain arid year\u2011round. Nights in the desert can be surprisingly cold, especially in winter.\nGujarat has a semi\u2011arid to tropical climate, with hot summers, a strong monsoon season, and mild winters. The coastal areas experience humidity and occasional cyclones, while the Rann of Kutch undergoes dramatic seasonal changes\u2014from dry salt flats in summer to shallow wetlands during the monsoon.\nMaharashtra\u2019s climate ranges from humid tropical conditions along the Konkan coast to drier, hotter weather on the Deccan Plateau. The Western Ghats (Sahyadri) receive heavy monsoon rainfall, creating lush forests and numerous waterfalls. Goa and the coastal union territories enjoy a warm, humid tropical climate with pronounced monsoon seasons and pleasant winters, making them popular year\u2011round destinations.\nCulture.\nWestern India is one of the most culturally diverse regions of the subcontinent, shaped by centuries of royal patronage, maritime trade, desert traditions, and colonial encounters. Each part of the region\u2014Rajasthan, Gujarat, Maharashtra, Goa, and Dadra and Nagar Haveli and Daman and Diu (DNHDD)\u2014has its own distinct identity, yet they share a common thread of craftsmanship, hospitality, and vibrant artistic expression. The cultural landscape is a blend of Hindu, Jain, Muslim, Christian, and tribal traditions, each contributing to a mosaic that feels both ancient and constantly evolving.\nRajasthan\u2019s culture is defined by its Rajput heritage, which permeates everything from architecture to music and social customs. Folk traditions such as Ghoomar, Kalbeliya, and the haunting melodies of Manganiyar musicians reflect a deep connection to the desert environment. Colour plays a central role in daily life\u2014bright turbans, embroidered garments, and painted havelis stand out against the stark backdrop of the Thar Desert. Storytelling, puppetry, and epic poetry preserve the region\u2019s martial history and romantic legends, while Jain and Hindu temples showcase exquisite stone carving and religious devotion.\nGujarat\u2019s cultural identity is shaped by its long history of trade and craftsmanship. The state is renowned for its textiles\u2014Bandhani tie\u2011dye, Patola silk weaving, mirror\u2011work embroidery, and block printing\u2014each associated with specific communities and regions. Gujarati cuisine reflects its mercantile and religious traditions, with vegetarian dishes, farsan snacks, and sweet\u2011savory combinations forming the heart of the culinary landscape. Festivals like Navaratri, celebrated with nine nights of Garba and Dandiya dancing, are among the most energetic cultural events in India. Coastal Gujarat also bears the imprint of centuries of maritime contact, visible in its Islamic architecture, Sufi shrines, and multicultural port towns.\nMaharashtra offers a different cultural rhythm, blending ancient traditions with modern urban dynamism. Marathi literature, theatre, and classical music have deep roots, while the Bhakti movement\u2014led by saints like Tukaram and Dnyaneshwar\u2014continues to influence spiritual life. The Konkan coast has its own distinct identity, shaped by coconut groves, fishing communities, and Konkani cuisine rich in seafood and spices. Mumbai, the region\u2019s largest city, is a cultural powerhouse: home to Bollywood, contemporary art, cosmopolitan nightlife, and a diverse population drawn from across India. Inland Maharashtra preserves older traditions through Lavani dance, Powada ballads, and the architectural legacy of the Marathas.\nGoa and DNHDD add a unique Indo\u2011Portuguese dimension to Western India\u2019s cultural tapestry. Centuries of Portuguese rule left behind baroque churches, tiled houses, fado music, and Catholic festivals that coexist with Hindu temples and local traditions. Goan cuisine blends Indian spices with European techniques, featuring dishes like vindaloo, sorpotel, and bebinca. Carnival, Christmas, and village feasts bring communities together in celebrations that feel distinctly different from the rest of India. In the smaller union territories, tribal communities such as the Warlis and Dhodias maintain their own artistic traditions, including the famous Warli painting style.\nTalk.\nWestern India is linguistically diverse, but travellers will find communication relatively easy thanks to the widespread use of Hindi and English in cities and tourist areas. Rajasthan\u2019s everyday speech is shaped by the Rajasthani dialects\u2014Marwari, Mewari, Shekhawati, and others\u2014though Hindi is commonly used in markets, hotels, and transport hubs. In rural areas, locals may speak mostly in their regional dialects, but they are generally accustomed to interacting with visitors.\nGujarat\u2019s primary language is Gujarati, spoken across the state in several regional varieties. Hindi is widely understood, especially in urban centres, and English is common in business, education, and tourism. Along the coast and in Kutch, travellers may also hear Kutchi, a language with strong Sindhi influences.\nMaharashtra is dominated by Marathi, though Mumbai and Pune are highly multilingual. In these cities, Marathi, Hindi, and English blend seamlessly in daily conversation, reflecting their cosmopolitan character. Along the Konkan coast and in Goa, Konkani is the main language, with noticeable differences between Hindu and Catholic communities. English is widely spoken in Goa, and Hindi is understood in most tourist areas. In Daman, Diu, and Dadra and Nagar Haveli, Gujarati mixes with tribal languages and Portuguese\u2011influenced dialects.\nGet in.\nFlying in is convenient, and there are many airports. Railway and sea links exist, but are not recommended for a number of reasons.\nBy plane.\nBy far the most convenient way to enter the region. There are many international airports and Mumbai is the busiest in the country.\nBy train.\nThere are no international options, given the Rajasthan\u2013Pakistan border and there are no trains between India and Pakistan since 2019.\nIndian Railways[dead link] trains connect Western India to many important cities in other parts of the country. Mumbai (CSMT, Central and LTT), Ahmedabad, Vadodara, Surat, Pune, Jaipur and Kolhapur (Chhatrapati Shahu Maharaj Terminus) are important railway hubs.\nThe Delhi Cantonment\u2013Ajmer Vande Bharat Express is the fastest train to Western India. The Delhi\u2013Mumbai Rajdhani Express connects the city to Mumbai in 18 hours. The Delhi\u2013Thiruvananthapuram Rajdhani Express also passes through Western India and is the fastest way of travelling through some parts of Gujarat.\nBy boat.\nCruises from the Mediterranean ports can be organized and are generally very expensive, amounting to well over \u20ac500 one-way. Mumbai is the busiest port in the country. Unless you have a lot of time to spare and a deep pocket, this option is not recommended. There are cheaper boats that ferry passengers from the UAE and Yemen to Mumbai.\nGet around.\nGetting around generally isn't a problem, as the transportation network is extensive.\nBy train.\nThere are good connections available by train. Mumbai is a hub for trains to all major cities. The Konkan Railway provides a picturesque route through the Western Ghats.\nBy road.\nThe national highway network is quite well maintained in Western India. The Mumbai\u2013Delhi leg of the Golden Quadrilateral provides excellent connectivity between Maharashtra, Gujarat and Rajasthan. Bus services are very frequent, particularly in Maharashtra and Gujarat. Online booking is possible, and there is no surcharge. They serve cities in Western India including Ahmedabad, Goa, Kolhapur, Mumbai, Nashik, Pune, Shirdi, Surat and Vadodara.\nOneway cab, CabBazar &amp; 7mcar.com Mumbai Pune Cab Service car ervice available in the western region.\nBy plane.\nWestern India is a large region, so you might want to fly long distances. Even within Western India, flights may well be over an hour long, the Pune-Ahmedabad flight takes one and a half hours. You have to check in an hour and a half in advance. There are numerous airlines serving Western India, for a comprehensive list see India#Get Around. The following airports are used for commercial purposes, there are numerous other small airfields that serve as training centers.\nSee and do.\nThere is plenty to see in Western India, which makes it a particularly touristy area.\nForts and palaces.\nThe spectacular forts of Rajasthan were built as unforgiving defensive citadels, but by the mid-16th century luxurious palaces, pleasure pavilions and gardens were added, displaying Mughal-inspired features. In the early 20th century there was another spate of palace buildings in Rajasthan and Gujarat. As a result of increasing contact with the British Raj, a marked European influence in both architecture and d\u00e9cor is clearly visible in these palaces.\nHill forts such as the ones in Chittorgarh and Jodhpur are the most impregnable examples of Rajasthani forts. The forts at Jaisalmer, on the other hand, lie amidst the sand dunes of the Thar Desert. Bharatpur's Lohagarh fort used thick mud walls for protection.\nThe Neemrana Fort Palace, now a hotel is another architectural wonder with brilliantly designed balconies and lush green patches.\nLalgarh Palace in Bikaner was built in 1902 and is a wonderful example of Indo-European architecture. The unique blend of both styles is reflected in the banquet halls and old-fashioned billiard rooms. The Wankaner Palace is crowned with Italianate pillars and Gothic arches. The cities of Jaipur and Udaipur have the best palaces.\nWildlife.\nWestern India has a variety of geography, ranging from the deserts of Rajasthan and Kutch-Gujarat to the long coast of Gujarat, Maharastra and Goa. There are some of the oldest forests of the Western Ghats and grasslands in Saurashtra-Gujarat and part of Maharashtra.\nSariska National Park and Ranthambore National Park are both home to tigers. However, the chances of spotting them are low (especially at Sariska since there are barely a handful left), unless you decide to spend a week or more at these places. The Keoladeo Bird Sanctuary on the outskirts of Bharatpur is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and is home to over 275 species of migrant birds. Don't expect miles and miles of forest green in these sanctuaries, most of the landscape is dry and slightly rocky scrubland.\nGir Forest National Park in Gujarat is the only place where you can find Asiatic Lions and their population is on the rise. Some other attractions for wildlife enthusiasts are Blackbuck National Park, Nalsarovar Bird Sanctuary, Wild Ass National Park in Kutch and Koyna Wildlife Sanctuary. There are many wildlife sanctuaries where approved campsites are available. Facilities at these campsites can vary widely and lesser-known sanctuaries have very basic camping facilities.\nMuseums.\nMumbai, Jaipur and Pune house a number of good museums. See the city pages for more information. The Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj Vastu Sangrahalaya and the National War Museum are perhaps the most well-known.\nEat.\nYou will find some great cuisine groups in the region. Gujarati cuisine is mostly vegetarian, sweet, and makes heavy use of milk products. Gujaratis make some of the best snack items such as the Dhokla and the Muthia. Rajasthani cuisine is similar to Gujarati, but somewhat spicier. Maharashtra and Goa are famous for their seafood.\nThalis.\nIf you order a thali (lit: \"platter\"), you will get a complete meal arranged on your plate, with a roti or chappati, rice and many different varieties of curries and curd. Ordering a thali is a popular option when you are hungry and in a hurry as it will usually be served blazingly fast. Most mid-level restaurants will have a thali on the menu, at least during lunch hours. Occasionally, they will be \"unlimited\", which means that some of the items are all-you-can-eat. The waiters will actually serve them at your table. Try the Gujarati thali, with its unlimited range of vegetarian dishes, or the spicier Rajasthani thali. The Rajdhani chain of restaurants serve the best thalis for a mere \u20b9124. They have numerous locations throughout Mumbai, Vadodara and Pune.\nDrink.\nThis is a region with drastically different approaches to alcohol! Gujarat is a \"dry state\", which means that you cannot legally purchase alcohol without a special permit. Dadra and Nagar Haveli and Daman and Diu, a union territory directly administered by the central government, has taken full advantage of Gujarat's prohibition by setting extremely low taxes on alcohol and as a result is a popular destination for Gujarati alcohol tourism. Goa has an extremely liberal attitude to drinking and \"feni\", a wine made of cashew is a popular drink there. Maharashtra is fast becoming a wine-producing region and the Sula wines are beginning to make a mark internationally.\n\"Bhang\", a form of cannabis is available at government-recognized shops and is socially accepted in Rajasthan.\nCafe Coffee Day and Barista have coffee stores in Mumbai, Pune, Vadodara, Ahmedabad, Gandhinagar, Rajkot, Jamnagar, Nashik, Jaipur, Jodhpur, Jaisalmer, Udaipur and Surat. Fresh juice is available off the sidewalk for nominal prices, usually \u20b915-\u20b920 a glass.\nThis region has tea-loving culture. Any time is good for offering tea. Tea here comes with milk (cream) and sugar unless you specify otherwise.\nStay safe.\nWestern India is generally safe and welcoming, but the region\u2019s size and varied landscapes mean conditions differ. Urban centres like Mumbai, Pune, Ahmedabad, Jaipur, and Panaji are busy and vibrant, so keep an eye on your belongings in crowded markets, railway stations, and festival areas. Petty theft is uncommon but possible in tourist hotspots, especially in Goa\u2019s beach zones and Rajasthan\u2019s major forts and bazaars.\nRoad safety requires attention. Traffic can be dense in cities and fast on highways, while rural roads may be narrow or poorly lit. Avoid driving at night in remote areas, and be cautious during the monsoon season, when heavy rain can cause flooding or slippery conditions, particularly along the Konkan coast and in parts of Gujarat. Rajasthan\u2019s desert regions can experience extreme heat; carry water, use sun protection, and avoid strenuous activity during the hottest hours.\nCoastal areas along the Arabian Sea\u2014especially in Goa and Maharashtra\u2014have strong currents at certain times of year. Swim only at beaches with lifeguards and heed local warnings. Wildlife encounters are possible near forests and national parks; keep a respectful distance from animals and avoid feeding them. Western India also borders Pakistan, but travellers rarely go near the international boundary, and the border zones are heavily controlled and generally off\u2011limits to visitors.\nAs always, trust your instincts, stay aware of your surroundings, and follow local advice during festivals, political gatherings, or large public events. With sensible precautions, most travellers find Western India a comfortable and rewarding region to explore."}
