{"id": "39219", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=39219", "title": "Whitefish", "text": "For the Wreck of the Edmund Fitzgerald museum, see Whitefish Point in Michigan. There is also a Whitefish Bay near Milwaukee, WI.\nWhitefish is a small town in the northern Flathead Valley of northwest Montana, on the southern end of Whitefish Lake. Whitefish Mountain Resort north of town offers excellent skiing, and Glacier National Park to the east is a major hiking and camping destination.\nUnderstand.\nWhitefish was founded in 1905 as a stopover for the Great Northern Railroad. Traditional industries in Whitefish have included logging, ranching, and farming. The population was about 8,300 in 2019.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nWhitefish is on U.S. Route 93 (US-93).\nFrom the south, take Exit 96 on Interstate 90 near Missoula and head north on US-93.\nFrom Calgary, the fastest route is to go southwest on the Crowsnest Highway 3, passing through Fernie. Continue south on Hwy 3 until it crosses with US-93. Take that south through the Roosville border crossing and the town of Eureka until hitting Whitefish. Driving time is about 5 hours.\nFrom Regina, take the Trans-Canada Hwy 1 west to Calgary and then follow the route explained above, which would take about 12-13 hours.\nAnother way is to take Hwy 6 south to Plentywood, and take Hwy 16 south to Culbertson. Take US-2 west through Glasgow and Havre until eventually hitting Whitefish. This takes about 13 hours.\nBy plane.\nFor commercial flights, the nearest airport is Glacier Park International Airport (), roughly 11.5 mi southeast of town on US-2 towards Kalispell. There is no public transportation available from the airport into town. It's either by rental car, taxi or ride sharing.\nThere's a small general aviation airport close to town:\nGet around.\nWalking, driving, or biking are ways to get around Whitefish.\nBuy.\nThe downtown area has many family-run stores. In the southern part are larger chain stores such as Safeway.\nThe commercial area south of the city in Kalispell on Highway 93 has all the national big-box chains you need.\nEat.\nBudget.\nFast food options in Whitefish include McDonald's, Taco John's, Wendy's, and Dairy Freeze.\nMid-range.\nAll of these options are in or within walking distance of the downtown area.\nSleep.\nThere are many lodging options available in and around Whitefish. They range from four star luxury, to budget, to vacation homes and condominiums."}
{"id": "39220", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=39220", "title": "Whitehall Township", "text": "Whitehall Township is a suburban township near Allentown in the Lehigh Valley region of eastern Pennsylvania. The township is 64.1 mi north of Philadelphia, the sixth-largest city in the United States, and 93.7 mi west of New York City, the nation's largest city.\nGet around.\nBy bus.\nIf you don't have a car of your own, the easiest way to get around is by LANta bus.\nSee.\nFor more things to do in the area, check out the Allentown, Bethlehem, or Easton pages.\nDo.\n[[File:Dorney Park Steel Force Thunderhawk.jpg|thumb|upright=1.3|Dorney Park &amp; Wildwater Kingdom's Steel Force and Thunderhawk roller coasters in neighboring Allentown. Steel Force is the eighth tallest steel roller coaster in the world with a first drop of 205 ft and a top speed of 75 mph. Dorney Park &amp; Wildwater Kingdom is located at 4000 Dorney Park Rd. in Allentown.\nWhitehall is primarily a shopping and dining town, centered around MacArthur Road. However, there are a few \"hidden\" things to do:\nFor more things to do in the Lehigh Valley, check out the Allentown, Bethlehem, and Easton pages.\nEat.\nDue to the abundance of shopping, restaurants line the main drag of Whitehall, MacArthur Road, and shopping centers. Dining options include:\nSleep.\nCheck out the listings on the Allentown page for many additional hotel options.\nConnect.\nPhones.\nIn Whitehall Township, like all Lehigh Valley locations, you must dial an area code when you place a call. Area codes in the Lehigh Valley are 610 and 484."}
{"id": "39221", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=39221", "title": "Whitehaven", "text": "\"For other places with the same name, see (disambiguation).\"\nWhitehaven is a town on the coast of Cumbria, in the North West region of England.\nGet in.\nThe town has two train stations: Whitehaven and Corkickle. Both of these stations are on the Barrow to Carlisle line, with a travel time of 1 hr 10 min to Carlisle. Access by road is by the A595 trunk road. Whilst the town was once a major port for England, the lack of a tributary to the harbour prevents modern vessels from docking. Occasionally historic tramp steamers will make calls to Whitehaven.\nGet around.\nThe town has two train stations Whitehaven and Corckickle, and is served by Stagecoach local services by far the easiest method of seeing the local attractions is by car.\nSee.\nWhitehaven was attacked by John Paul Jones - founder of the US Navy - during the American revolutionary wars. Many of the cannon which were in the fort at the time are still in the harbour area. However, they were reused in the 1950s as road bollards and are now implanted in the pavements to separate it from the road. The town is also the first in the UK to be laid out in a grid format, and was later used as a template for major cities.\nThe town's cemetery is also home to the grandmother of George Washington, the first president of the USA.\nThe church at the centre of town is worth a visit. The original church was burned down, and due to funding issues rather than rebuild, the saved part was bricked off from the remaining destroyed part and now forms the church.\nOne day should be sufficient to see all the town has to offer."}
